+# parcel-tracker
+
+Shipment tracking API: carriers post webhooks, drivers report progress from the app,
+ops staff use the admin routes.
+
+## Conventions
+- All configuration is read in `src/config.ts`. No `process.env` reads anywhere else.
+- Shipment status changes go through `transition()` in `src/shipments/transition.ts`. Never
+  `update shipments set status = ...` directly.
+- Read `docs/lessons-learned.md` before touching outbound calls or admin routes.
+- We run 3 API instances behind the load balancer; anything in memory is per instance.
diff --git a/docs/lessons-learned.md b/docs/lessons-learned.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..12b6f1a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/lessons-learned.md
@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@
+# Lessons learned
+
+## 2025-03-14 — Outbound calls inside transactions
+We held row locks on `shipments` while calling the carrier API inside a transaction. When the
+carrier slowed down, every driver report queued behind those locks and the app stalled for
+40 minutes. **Rule:** never make a network call inside a database transaction. Claim the row,
+commit, make the call, then record the result in a second short transaction.
+
+## 2025-06-02 — Admin actions
+A new admin endpoint shipped without being added to `ADMIN_ACTIONS`, and any logged-in user
+could call it. **Rule:** every admin action must be listed in `ADMIN_ACTIONS` in
+`src/admin/routes.ts`; the guard only protects what is in that list.
